A public method needs a new `timeout` parameter. Adding it as a required parameter breaks every existing call site immediately:

```typescript
// breaking — every existing call site now fails to compile
function fetchUser(id: string, timeout: number): User { /* ... */ }
```

Three additive-safe alternatives, depending on the language:

```typescript
// 1. default parameter — existing calls keep working unchanged
function fetchUser(id: string, timeout: number = 5000): User { /* ... */ }

// 2. overload — old signature still resolves, new one is opt-in
function fetchUser(id: string): User;
function fetchUser(id: string, timeout: number): User;

// 3. options object — scales past one or two new parameters
function fetchUser(id: string, options?: { timeout?: number }): User { /* ... */ }
```

A default parameter is simplest for one addition; an options object scales better once you expect to keep adding parameters — you never need another breaking change to add the next one.
